Generally, plants employ signature enzymes (e.g., cyclases) to form the basic carbon backbones, followed by tailoring enzymes (e.g., P450s, acyltransferases, and glycosyltransferases) that adds specific functional groups to form various metabolites. Among these biocatalysts, the P450 superfamily is the most versatile, participating in irreversible and oftentimes rate-limiting reactions including regio- and stereoselective hydroxylation, as well as complex reactions including epoxidation, sulfoxidation, arylaryl coupling, nitration, and oxidative and reductive dehalogenations (Guengerich and Munro, 2013). Because of their pivotal roles in the biosynthesis of plant-specialized products, P450s are of great interest and selected as essential components for the metabolic engineering of plants (Urlacher and Girhard, 2019). As several reviews have addressed the progress made on genome mining and functional characterization of plant P450s, this review will focus on the topic of P450 engineering and its potential application in the metabolic engineering of plant-derived natural products.
## The importance of modulating P450s in plant metabolite engineering
Enzymes with high catalytic efficiency and selectivity are crucial to the reconstruction of metabolic pathways in plants or heterologous hosts. Unfortunately, studies have shown that plant-specialized metabolism is inherently slow when compared to that of  the central metabolism (Bar-Even et al., 2011). There has been speculation that this is due to central metabolism being more conserved across phylogenetic groups, and thus the related
1 enzymes tend to be better optimized during evolution. On the other hand, secondary   
2 metabolism aids in the adaptation to changing environments, which requires the   
3 associated enzymes to be more flexible regarding substrate recognition and product   
4 spectrum at the cost of lower catalytic rates (Nam et al., 2012). This is the case for plant   
5 P450s, which usually exhibit low $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{cat}}<5\mathrm{~\textsubscript~{~s~}~}^{-1}$ and are well-known for their   
6 substrate promiscuity (Bar-Even and Tawfik, 2013; Bernhardt and Urlacher, 2014). In   
7 addition to low rates and poor specificities, plant P450s have two other characteristics   
8 that hinder their widespread use in engineering applications. First of all, these enzymes   
9 tend to be incompatible with non-native hosts and statistical data suggests that ${\sim}40\%$ of   
10 plant P450s are poorly expressed in yeast (Renault et al., 2014). Secondly, the electron   
11 flux from $\mathrm{\DeltaNAD(P)H}$ , the ultimate electron source in cells, to P450s is usually mediated   
12 by an additional redox partner such as cytochrome P450 reductases (CPR). This   
13 introduces several more optimization requirements, as the inefficient electron transfer   
14 resulting from either the lack of sufficient NAD(P)H levels or the poor interactions   
15 between P450s and CPRs would reduce the enzymatic activity (Lundemo and Woodley,   
16 2015).   
17 An emerging area of P450 engineering is to create abiotic reactions for the de novo   
18 synthesis of chemical products and their derivatives. In the plant genome, approximately   
19 $1\%$ of the coding genes are P450s (Nelson and Werck-Reichhart, 2011). Although recent   
20 advances have been made in genome mining using multi-omics data to accelerate the   
21 identification of enzymatic pathways, this process is still tedious and challenging. For   
22 example, the biosynthetic pathway of taxol, an anti-cancer agent originally isolated from   
23 the bark of pacific yew trees, has not been not fully elucidated. The missing steps from   
24 taxa-4(5),11(12)-diene- $\cdot10\upbeta$ -ol-5α-acetate to 10-deacetylbaccan III are thought to   
25 be catalyzed by P450s (Kusari et al., 2014). Based on the fact that intermediates produced   
26 in the taxol pathway should be structurally similar, this information could be utilized to   
27 search for the missing P450s from genomic data of yew trees using the method described   
28 by Zhao et al (Zhao et al., 2013). In addition, it is also possible to repurpose two known   
29 P450s in the taxol pathway, T5αH and T10βH, that have structurally similar substrates to   
30 that of the missing P450s (Schoendorf et al., 2001; Jennewein et al., 2004), thereby   
31 completing the pathway in producing taxol (Figure 1). Apart from pathway gap-filling,
functionally modifying plant P450s could be used to add functional groups to high-value metabolites to mitigate their unwanted side-effects (e.g. cytotoxicity). For instance, gossypol, a sesquiterpene aldehyde that accumulates in cotton seeds, has been demonstrated to exhibit anti-tumor activity against several cancer cells (Neergheen et al., 2010), but there is also evidence pointing to its anti-nutritional properties (Sunilkumar et al., 2006). Fortunately, several related intermediates with potentially similar bioactivities that are less toxic have been identified recently (Tian et al., 2018, 2019).In these studies, it was shown that cotton uses CYP71BE79 as a highly active enzyme to decorate one of the intermediates (8-hydroxy-7-keto-δ-cadinene) to prevent its phytotoxicity during gossypol synthesis (Tian et al., 2018, 2019). Interestingly, while genes involved in secondary metabolic pathways tend to be clustered in the plant genome (Field et al., 2011; Chae et al., 2014), the gossypol pathway genes are dispersed, and the three P450s identified belong to different families, suggesting that phylogenetically distant P450s can also act on structurally similar substrates (Tian et al., 2018).
## Protein engineering of P450s and their applications
Rational design and directed evolution are the commonly used strategies in protein engineering (Chen and Zeng, 2016). Rational design uses physics-based energy functions calculated from models or crystal structures of proteins to guide modifications that alter the substrate specificity, structural stability, or kinetic property of enzymes. As conformational dynamism of a protein provides a basis for it to acquire new functions (Tokuriki and Tawfik, 2009), qualitatively accurate computing methods for the protein energy landscape is required for an efficient rational design (Ranganathan, 2018). In addition, as some protein families such as P450s are notoriously difficult to crystallize, accurate protein modeling methods are also important. On the other hand, directed evolution is a nature-inspired protein engineering approach originally developed in the early 1990s (Chen and Arnold, 1993). The basic logic of this approach is to generate sequence diversity, then iteratively select variants with desired properties. As nature has spent millions of years to optimize protein functions in different species, successful application of directed evolution largely relies on the ability to generate diverse protein sequences and to select proper variants using high-throughput systems
1 (Jung et al., 2011; Bassalo et al., 2016). A number of new enzymes, especially P450s,   
2 with novel functions have been created through this approach (Arnold, 2015).
## Application one: To improve enzyme-host compatibility
5 In terms of engineering plant P450s, one major challenge lies in improving its   
6 enzyme-host compatibility, as P450s involved in the biosynthesis of plant-specialized   
7 products are membrane-bound and different from the soluble cytoplasmic P450s in   
8 prokaryotes (Munro et al., 2007). To this end, the residues within the N-terminal   
9 transmembrane anchor region of eukaryotic P450s could be deleted or modified to   
10 improve protein solubility without affecting their activity. The first crystal structure of a   
11 mammalian P450 (CYP2C5) was obtained by applying this method (Williams et al.,   
12 2000). Moreover, plant P450s involved in secondary metabolism are usually poorly   
13 expressed in yeast or bacteria. Thus, the promoter, codon usage, as well as copy number   
14 also need to be optimized to increase the yield of desired compounds (Figure 2A). For   
15 example, an induced promoter with a specific codon version were used in a study to   
16 successfully achieve a higher P450 expression which resulted in a $35\%$ increase in the   
17 production of perillyl alcohol in bacteria (Alonso-Gutierrez et al., 2013). Similar   
18 strategies including codon optimization and adding additional gene copies have been used   
19 to enhance the expression of a plant P450 (G8H) to increase the production titer of   
20 strictosidine in yeast (Brown et al., 2015). In addition to microbial platforms, using   
21 tobacco as an efficient plant-based host represents an alternative way to optimize the   
22 expression of plant P450s (Reed et al., 2017). Finally, substrate specificity is another   
23 factor that affects the metabolic flux toward target compounds. Although a broad   
24 substrate specificity of P450s increases its chance to evolve new functions, this substrate   
25 promiscuity could impose a kinetic barrier that blocks the synthesis of desired products,   
26 thus requiring substrate recognition site engineering. For instance, a 5.6-fold higher   
27 activity was achieved for Coleus forskohlii CYP76AH15 by tuning its substrate   
28 preference (Forman et al., 2018).
## 30 Application two: To optimize electron flux during oxidation reaction
1 Most eukaryotic P450s are membrane-bound proteins mainly expressed in the   
2 endoplasmic reticulum (ER) (Werck-Reichhart and Feyereisen, 2000). The lipid   
3 composition and amount of ER in cells determines the localization as well as expression   
4 of ER-resident P450 enzymes, which in turn affects their behaviors (Strobel et al., 1970;   
5 Negretti et al., 2014; Park et al., 2014). For instance, oleaginous yeasts that are rich in   
6 membrane compartments provide an ideal environment for introducing plant-derived   
7 P450s in the biosynthesis of flavonoids (Lv et al., 2019). Another way to improve the   
8 activity of P450s is to enhance the physical proximity between a P450 enzyme and its   
9 redox partner on the ER membranes, leading to better electron flux during oxidation   
10 reactions (Figure 2B). This was demonstrated previously when the P450 isoflavone   
11 synthase 1 from Glycine max was fused to the bacterial P450 reductase domain from   
12 Rhodococcus sp. to form the first active P450 fusion enzyme (Sabbadin et al., 2010).   
13 Optimization of the taxol precursor production by engineering the N-terminal sequence of   
14 T5αH and linking this enzyme with a CPR from Taxus represents another elegant   
15 example (Ajikumar et al., 2010). Although both activity and solubility of plant P450s   
16 could be increased through protein fusion, introduction of these artificial enzymes into   
17 host cells may lead to uncoupled electron transfers or excessive production of reactive   
18 oxygen species, both of which are toxic to the cells (Renault et al., 2014). As such,   
19 balancing the expression ratio between P450 and CPR is another way to efficiently   
20 optimize the electron transfer process while mitigating the aforementioned issues. To   
21 demonstrate the effectiveness of this idea, a cytochrome ${\sf b}_{5}$ was introduced into yeast with   
22 low CPR and normal CYP71AV1 expression levels. The results showed that both   
23 yeast viability and the yield of artemisinic acid, the precursor to artemisinin, were   
24 improved (Paddon et al., 2013). The importance of regulating the electron transfer   
25 process by cytochrome $\mathbf{b}_{5}$ can also be seen in engineering flower color. For instance,   
26 overexpression of a cytochrome ${\sf b}_{5}$ and CYP75A is required for the accumulation of blue   
27 pigment in carnation (Tanaka and Brugliera, 2013), whereas a pink color is achieved by   
28 the down-regulation of CYP75B in cyclamen flowers (Boase et al., 2010).
## 30 Application three: To create abiotic reactions for P450s
1 Currently, engineering P450s to catalyze abiotic reactions is a field of primary   
2 interest. The catalytic cycle and the reactive intermediates produced during the process   
3 are essential to the activity of a P450 enzyme (Krest et al., 2013). Among these reactive   
4 intermediates, the iron-oxo species, also known as compound I, tend to abstract   
5 hydrogens from substrates, which favor proton-coupled oxidation (Green, 2009); the   
6 iron-peroxo, or hydroperoxide, works as an oxidant in epoxidation, sulfoxidation, and C  
7 C cleavage reactions (Jin et al., 2003; Cryle and De Voss, 2006); ferric-superoxide   
8 species can mediate nitration reactions (Barry et al., 2012). Residue mutations that affect   
9 either formation or stabilization of these reactive intermediates would result in novel   
10 P450s reactions (Figure 2C). As an example, the universally conserved cysteine among   
11 P450s is involved in ligating the heme iron from the axial side. Mutating this important   
12 residue to serine abolishes the native monooxygenase activity of CYP102A1 (P450 BM3)   
13 and enables the variant with new catalytic abilities such as C-H amination and   
14 cyclopropanation reactions (Coelho et al., 2013a; Wang et al., 2014). Through this   
15 cysteine mutation as well as other residue reforms, researchers have introduced additional   
16 reactions including sulfimidation to generate S-N bonds (Farwell et al.,   
17 2014), aziridination of aryl olefins (Farwell et al., 2015), and carbene transfer to alkynes   
18 (Chen et al., 2018) to P450 BM3. Another conserved residue is threonine located within   
19 the I-helix on the distal side of the heme that usually acts as a proton shuttle for O-O   
20 bond scission to produce compound I (Krest et al., 2013). Olefin cyclopropanation   
21 activity was introduced to P450 BM3 by mutations that include this residue (Coelho et al.,   
22 2013b). Currently, engineering P450s to create de novo reactions primarily focuses on   
23 enzymes originating from microbes and mammals with very few successful examples   
24 related to plant enzymes. Due to the limited understanding of P450s (e.g. enzyme   
25 dynamics), the targeted regio- and stereo-electivity of plant P450 engineering still   
26 remains a major challenge.
## Toolboxes and perspectives for engineering plant P450s
Protein engineering approaches, especially those developed based on directed evolution, require the capacity to generate a large number of sequences that are usually achieved by ep-PCR (Cadwell and Joyce, 1992), site-directed mutagenesis (Wang and Malcolm,
1 1999), multiplex automated genome engineering (Wang et al., 2009), or   
2 genome shuffling (Zhang et al., 2002). With the advances in DNA synthesis technology   
3 and decreases in associated costs, direct synthesis of DNA sequences has become much   
4 easier to introduce sequence mutagenesis, optimize codon usage for specific hosts, or   
5 piece together gene elements as a start point for protein engineering. However, the   
6 increased ability to create diverse sequences also requires the corresponding high  
7 throughput screening methods for the selection process. Allosteric transcription factor or   
8 RNA-based biosensors using either fluorescent proteins or antibiotic resistance genes as   
9 reporters have recently been developed and applied to accelerate protein engineering   
10 (Bassalo et al., 2016; Wan et al., 2019). In addition, as cell dependent systems   
11 have several innate drawbacks, cell-free screening methods such as droplet or   
12 microchamber-based platforms have been developed as efficient alternatives (Longwell et   
13 al., 2017). Overall, applications of these ultra-high-throughput technologies could   
14 accelerate protein engineering and transform it into a less painstaking process.
As the cost for next-generation DNA sequencing technology has dramatically decreased, a tremendous amount of sequence data have been generated by big genome sequencing projects such as 10KP (10,000 Plants) Genome Sequencing Project (Cheng et al., 2018) and metagenome sequence projects (Rigden and Fernández, 2017). This has provided unprecedented opportunities to study both protein co-evolution among different species and co-varying residues within various proteins through the combined application of different statistical analyses (De Juan et al., 2013), giving insights into the strategies used by nature to create the functional domains as well as residues involved in substrate binding, catalysis, and allosteric regulation. With the constantly growing pool of genomic data (>350,000 P450 sequences available in databases) (Nelson, 2018) and the development of new statistical analysis tools such as machine learning for data mining (Yang et al., 2019), the predicted functional domains or co-varying residues revealed from these analyses are becoming more and more accurate, thus narrowing the choice of beneficial mutations to a much smaller pool, thereby accelerating protein engineering.
De novo computational protein design is an approach to introduce new structure or function to enzymes. The software Rosetta, developed in David Baker’s lab, is one of the most popular computational tools for protein design. This method integrates protein
1 structure, folding, binding, and assembly with powerful computing of all-atom energy   
2 functions to create novel proteins that have extremely high stability (Kellogg et al.,   
3 2011). As suggested by the de novo protein design, the lowest free energy state of a   
4 protein should determine its conformation (Baker, 2019). This core principle could be   
5 used to create stable enzyme variants that serve as a start pointing to incorporate a greater   
6 number of simultaneous mutations during engineering. In addition, Rosetta structure   
7 calculation combined with co-varying residue-residue contacts could be applied to   
8 accurately predict protein structure (Ovchinnikov et al., 2017), which is extremely useful   
9 for rational redesign of plant P450s that are hard to crystallize or lack homologs of known   
10 structures for protein modeling. As a proof of concept, Rosetta as well as the information   
11 of co-evolving amino acids were utilized to engineer a multifunctional P450 (CYP87D20)   
12 into a monooxygenase (Li et al., 2019), aiming to create a de novo pathway to direct the   
13 metabolic flux from cucurbitacin C (a bitter triterpenoid from cucumber) towards mogrol   
14 (the precursor to the natural sweetener mogroside in Siraitia grosvenorii) (Figure 2D).
Although progress has been made in the identification and engineering of plant P450s to accelerate research related to plant natural products, there is still a large gap between academic research and industrial applications. Nature has created a diverse and fascinating world of enzymes that remains largely unexplored, waiting to be better understood, uncovered, and utilized. The emerging new tools, especially DNA synthesis, machine learning, and de novo computational protein design, combined with the massive amount of DNA sequencing data of P450s, could be integrated to accelerate the engineering of such enzymes (Figure 3). This will eventually lead to better usage of plant specialized metabolites in various applications.
