Figure 1. Engineering of P450s to create novel reactions and its potential applications. The missing steps within the taxol metabolic pathway is indicated with a dotted line and P450s are suggested to be involved in these steps. Two pathway P450s, T5αH and $\mathrm{T10\betaH}$ , sharing substrate similarities with the missing P450s (indicated with red backbone) are suitable for engineered to acquire new activities that can fill the pathway gap.
Figure 2. Successful cases that improved enzyme-host compatibility (A) and activities of the P450s (B), as well as conferred abiotic reactions to the P450s, creating de novo metabolites (C) or metabolic pathways (D). Cuol, cucurbitadienol; C, carbonyl; H, hydroxyl.
Figure 3. A workflow of plant P450 engineering using integrated tools in the future. Metagenome data is explored using machine learning to identify the co-evolving amino acids of P450s. This information is adopted by Rosetta structure prediction to generate high accurate P450 models. Sequences of protein variants generated from direct evolution or rational design are further optimized by Rosetta to improve protein stability, and then directly synthesized for the following sensor-assisted screening process. Iterative optimization may be needed to acquire desired properties for P450s.
